        <mxCell id="l7wsbRFqEFvn701YVKcY-44" parent="1" style="text;whiteSpace=wrap;" value="We upload a CSV file with raw data to the bucket raw-file-pipeline-nak76700 on S3.&#xa;&#xa;Each time a file is uploaded, a Lambda function is automatically triggered and processes the raw data by reformatting and cleaning it, and creates a new processed file in another S3 bucket: processed-file-pipeline-nak76700.&#xa;&#xa;In case a file is not validated, Lambda sent an event to a DLQ for manual inspection&#xa;&#xa;CloudWatch monitors the DLQ and if an event is detected in the DLW, triggers an Alarm&#xa;&#xa;The alarm is notified to a SNS topic to trigger a mail. &#xa;&#xa;An AWS Glue Crawler automatically scans the CSV file to detect the structure, then creates the schema (tables, columns, data types) in the AWS Glue Data Catalog.&#xa;&#xa;The AWS Glue Data Catalog, which acts as a centralized metadata store, records this schema as a table that will be used by a Glue ETL job.&#xa;&#xa;An AWS Glue job retrieves the data, transforms it, and loads the final version of the file into a new S3 bucket: final-file-pipeline-nak76700.&#xa;&#xa;Amazon QuickSight connects to the final dataset and presents the dashboard for data visualization.&#xa;&#xa;To ensure that the Glue Crawler has finished its job before the Glue job start, we need to integrate it in the Step Function workflow. For that, " vertex="1">
          <mxGeometry height="410" width="591" x="21" y="810" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
